  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 AM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nTODAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY...Really not a lot to talk about in\nterms of weather for central Minnesota and western Wisconsin, as\nwe remain in between the storm track to the south and storm\ntrack to the north.\n\nEarly morning satellite imagery showed a few high clouds across\nthe region from the developing cyclone over the central and\neastern US. Meanwhile radar showed some light returns across the\nDakotas ahead of an upper level trough, but this system was\nmoisture-starved with cloud bases above 10kft, and no\nprecipitation being reported at the ground.\n\nFor today, expect light winds and clear skies to the west with\nclouds to the east. Temperatures will be mild for late\nNovember, with highs generally near 40.\n\nOn Friday, and weak frontal boundary will push eastward across\nthe region. Did include some small chances (20 to 30 percent)\nfor light rain or snow showers. Any amounts will be less than a\ntenth of an inch.\n\nLooking ahead, another frontal boundary will pass through dry on\nSaturday, but this will bring cooler air across the region.\nExpect northwest winds to increase Saturday evening, with gusts\nof 25 to 30 mph overnight. These breezy conditions will continue\non Sunday, with highs only in the 30s. \n\nLooking ahead, the southern stream will keep the moisture and\nstorms south of the Upper Midwest. Meanwhile the northern stream\nwill develop some cyclones across southern Canada, but the best\nwe'll be able to do is small chances for rain or snow showers.\nIn summary, there are no major storm systems on the horizon\nanytime soon.",
